  "assumptions": [
    "Idealized small-swing model: period T = 2π√(L/g) with g = 9.81 m/s², exact in the model and only approximately true of a real pendulum.",
    "The string is light and doesn't stretch, the bob is a point mass, and there is no air resistance or friction, so the swing never dies down.",
    "The swing amplitude is fixed at 10°, small enough that the formula is a close approximation.",
    "The swing angle is computed from the timeline time, so changing the length makes the swing jump to the matching point of the new motion rather than continuing smoothly; the 60 s loop also restarts the swing."
  ],
  "program": {
    "source": "const G=9.81,A=10*Math.PI/180;\nexport function evaluate({inputs,time}){const L=Math.min(3,Math.max(0.5,Number(inputs.length)||1));const T=2*Math.PI*Math.sqrt(L/G);const th=A*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*time/T);const h=th/2;return{nodes:{pivot:{quaternion:[0,0,Math.sin(h),Math.cos(h)]},str:{position:[0,-L/2,0],scale:[1,L,1]},bob:{position:[0,-L,0]}},readouts:{period:T,len:L}};}"
  }
